Below you’ll find two quick patterns—one for **functional** components (using `useState`) and one for classic **class‑based** components—to “clear” your `userSession` state value back to `null`.

---

### 1️⃣ Functional Component (`React Hooks`)

```tsx
import { useState } from 'react';

function MyComponent() {
  const [userSession, setUserSession] = useState<any | null>(/* initial value */);

  /* …other logic… */

  /** Reset the session */
  const handleLogout = () => {
    setUserSession(null);          // ← clears the stored session data
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Render UI based on whether there's a session */}
      {userSession ? (
        <>
          <p>Welcome!</p>
          <button onClick={handleLogout}>Log out</button>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>No active session.</p>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

*Tip:* If you’re storing more than just the raw session token (e.g., an entire user profile), consider setting the whole object to `null`, e.g.:

```ts
setUserSession({
  ...initialEmptyProfileObject,
});
```
but usually clearing to `null` signals *no logged‑in user*.

---

### 2️⃣ Class Component

```jsx
class MyComponent extends React.Component<{}, { userSession: any | null }> {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { userSession: /* initial value */ };
  }

  /* …other methods… */

  /** Reset the session */
  logoutHandler = () => {
    this.setState({ userSession: null });   // ← clears the stored session data
  };

  render() {
    const { userSession } = this.state;
    return (
      <div>
        {userSession ? (
          <>
            <p>Welcome!</p>
            <button onClick={this.logoutHandler}>Log out</button>
          </>
        ) : (
          <p>No active session.</p>
        )}
      </div>
    );
  }
}
```

---

#### Why `null`?

Using `null` instead of an empty object/string makes it explicit that there’s *no current session*. Many libraries (like Redux selectors or context consumers) treat `null` as “not authenticated”, whereas an empty object could still pass truthy checks.

---

#### Bonus – Using Context / Global State

If `userSession` lives in a global store (Redux, Zustand, Recoil, etc.), dispatch the appropriate action/trigger to set it to `null`. For instance, with Redux:

```js
dispatch(setUserSession(null));     // assuming you exported such an action creator
```

Or with React Context + reducer pattern:

```js
dispatch({ type: 'RESET_SESSION', payload: null });
```

---

That’s all! Just replace whatever placeholder values I’ve put in with your actual implementation details. Happy coding 🚀
